## Theorem scope and model adaptation

The additional explicit link amplitudes, charge changes, Gauss constraint and
cube compensation agree with the supplied model used in PRE and POST. On
this cube the occupancy gates make C_S vanish outside W=0; within W=0 its
stated PF*FP+D/C formula is the same compensation. The birth signs and spin
boundary amplitudes are unchanged. The canonical zero-field preparation,
actual first-mark normalization, original resolved/coherent instruments and
zero terminal Hamiltonian remain explicit.

The moving lower endpoint is valid for every a_epsilon>=0 satisfying
a_epsilon/epsilon^2->infinity. For each fixed finite tau, eventually
epsilon^2 tau<a_epsilon. Exact contraction of the commuting oblique cluster
component then compares every t>=a_epsilon with that one finite fast time.
The joint limsup is taken before sending tau to infinity. This is the same
argument already present in the independent PRE, with no growing-time
perturbative estimate introduced in publication.

The publication correctly separates the moment scopes:

- The scaled mean is controlled on [a_epsilon,T], with T fixed and
  a_epsilon<=T eventually. Its low-band argument uses the compact
  physical-time density limit against uniformly bounded Q_S.
- The scaled second moment and variance are controlled for every
  t>=a_epsilon, without an upper bound. Their low-band contribution is
  uniformly O(epsilon^2) by the operator bound on Q_S and exact contraction,
  so this conclusion needs no density limit on an unbounded time interval.

The proof therefore does not extend the mean's compact-time density estimate
to all future times. The stated failure of uniformity at birth, absence of an
unscaled bound or rate, and dependence on fixed positive delta,K,kappa remain
correct. The possible second birth is included throughout. The status and
machine block distinguish a proposed bounded result from an applied retained
audit grade.

